\subsection{Why these anatomies are reported separately}\label{sec:S10.1}

The MRI check (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}) classed the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>. Its white surface fitted the T1 contrast best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift, with an edge translation of << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_edge_translation_mm >> mm, both beyond the << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >>-mm tolerance (other templates' best shift, << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >> mm). Whether this reflects a displaced surface or the weaker contrast of an average image was not settled, and the surface was not repaired, so the template's results may rest on a displaced white surface.

The children needed three further steps (Sections~\ref{sec:S4.1} and \ref{sec:S4.3}). Their inner skull, placed by the dataset a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below the scalp over the upper head, was moved inward; their fiducials were transferred from the adult; and their surfaces were checked against their T1 images. The check found the surfaces registered to the images and the modeled scalp about as far inside the T1 head boundary as the adult's (<% if F.rev_qc_n_children_usable == F.g3b_n_children_heads %>all << F.g3b_n_children_heads|words >> children passed the check<% else %><< F.rev_qc_n_children_usable|words >> of the << F.g3b_n_children_heads|words >> children passed the check<% endif %>). It cannot detect a head boundary that the images themselves place too deep, settle whether the white surface is anatomically correct within a few millimeters of the scalp, or test tissue thicknesses against norms for age.

How many targets lie near the scalp depends on the scalp used: child B had << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> targets shallower than << F.g3b_shallow_target_limit_mm >> mm below the modeled scalp but << F.wr_qc_child_b_targets_lt10mm_mri_n >> below the MRI head boundary. The source rule, applied at the modeled inner skull, left out << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the children's white-surface area within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the scalp, so their results do not describe a near-scalp cortex (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}).

With a single-compartment BEM, which has no skull or scalp conductivity but keeps the modeled inner skull as its boundary, the difference between each of these heads' median $D$ and the adult's changed by << F.meth_provisional_bem1_change_db_range >> dB (Section~\ref{sec:S4.1}). The skull and scalp layers thus do not carry these differences; the check does not validate the location of the inner skull, which both models share.

